Separate continuity from a drawing or a list of observations (@math-continuity-models: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Define-a-derivative-as-a-justified-local-rate.lingua#r_02335A93A6AF69D38C38651917") { r_0C8135C0FD2CA421CC59E691E2
At an interior point a, continuity means that f(a) is defined and lim_(x→a)f(x)=f(a). The output at the point and the nearby outputs agree in the tolerance sense. On a closed interval [a,b], require continuity at interior points and the appropriate one-sided agreement at endpoints. This is a property of a stated function on a stated domain, not a conclusion obtained by drawing a smooth line through a few observations.

The previous square-function estimate proves continuity of x² everywhere. A finite sum of continuous functions is continuous: allocate a requested output error among the finitely many terms and take the smallest input radius. For a product, use fg−AB=f(g−B)+B(f−A), where A and B are the limiting values, and first bound f near A. This explains why the product limit needs local boundedness rather than an unjustified multiplication of approximate numbers. Quotients additionally require a denominator staying away from zero; continuity and a nonzero denominator value give such a neighborhood.

The modified quotient g with g(3)=20 fails continuity because its limit is 6. Setting g(3)=6 repairs this one removable discontinuity. The step j at 1 cannot be repaired by assigning a different value at that point, because its one-sided limits disagree. The absolute-value function is continuous: ||x|−|a||≤|x−a|, so δ=ε suffices. It will nevertheless fail to have a two-sided derivative at zero. Continuity and differentiability are different conditions.

Two foundational real-number theorems will support the next arguments. A continuous real function on a closed bounded interval attains its minimum and maximum there. Also, it takes every value between its endpoint values. Read their exact hypotheses in [Calculus and differential-equation preparation — Strang and Herman, OpenStax](../../chemistry/industrial-chemistry/sources/Calculus-and-Differential-Equations-OpenStax.lingua#r_027WKVM5NG5X84M86TSVRA77J3), Volume 1 sections 4.3 and 2.4. Their general proofs rely on the completeness properties of the real numbers and belong to later analysis; identify these theorems as used foundations rather than pretending a picture proves them. We will prove the subsequent derivative/accumulation connections from explicitly stated premises.

The hypotheses matter. The function x on (0,1) has neither an attained minimum nor an attained maximum, even though it is continuous there. The step j on [0,2] has endpoint values 0 and 2 but never equals 1. A continuity theorem cannot fill this gap because the function is discontinuous. A polynomial with opposite signs at two endpoints has a root between them by the intermediate-value theorem, but that alone neither locates the root exactly nor proves it unique.

**Work:** classify each earlier example by domain, point value, one-sided limits and continuity. Give an explicit tolerance proof for |x|. Propose two functions agreeing at a finite list of recorded points but behaving differently between them. Explain the extra modeling assumption needed before interpolation becomes a physical claim, using [Design comparisons that can distinguish competing explanations](../../inquiry/core/Design-comparisons-that-can-distinguish-competing-explanations.lingua#r_0A7E63167B1C0C05400C73A84E) and [Separate a prediction from a fitted description](../../physics/core/Separate-a-prediction-from-a-fitted-description.lingua#r_07A97F390AC6E5D127D08C8E42). Continuous mathematical models can be useful approximations; discrete stock events and abrupt protocol changes require their own descriptions.

